# T22655 — modbus — dynamic changes — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-29 — PASS

Verdict: PASS. All four steps confirmed: a TCP-port change takes effect immediately (the old
port refuses, the new one answers); the server can be disabled and re-enabled dynamically;
a port can be disabled and enabled through the Modbus API; a CLI `shutdown` is reflected in
the port registers. Run labelled: clean. One observation (not a failed step): on an
AGGREGATOR-MEMBER port the API write returns exception 4 (slave device failure) although the
enable/disable is applied — see step 3.

Operator: bench-runner subagent (device-testing-61 = sentinel; Terrence away). Queue row 8,
case 3 of 6. Hand-driven: ckcon.py on /dev/u5 + mb.py (pymodbus 3.8.6) from tb470 eth1
10.38.215.1 → stack vlan1 10.38.215.10. Full stdout 22655.out, client log mb-22655.log,
driver 22655.sh. DUT clock ≈ tb470 − 12 h 50 min.

## Case (ck.db AWPTCM-T22655)
Precondition: modbus configured and client able to retrieve information. Steps: 1 dynamic
change tcp port (client has to change its port before it can connect again); 2 dynamic
enable and disable modbus; 3 using API to disable and enable a port; 4 using CLI to disable a
port (modbus retrieves the port state correctly).

## Precondition (13:59:09)
`scada modbus tcp server` + `scada modbus tcp server access read-write` → "MODBUS is enabled /
Write access: enabled / Port 502"; client READ 0x0000 x1 unit 0 -> 5 (mapping version) on 502.
Pre-test config = pre-test-configs/u5.show_running-config.txt.

## STEP 1 — dynamic TCP port change (13:59:24)
`scada modbus tcp server port 5020` → `show scada modbus tcp server | include Port`: "Port : 5020";
running-config: `scada modbus tcp server port 5020` (plus access read-write + server).
  CONNECT 10.38.215.10:502  -> FAILED (Connection refused)          ← the old port
  CONNECT 10.38.215.10:5020 -> ok; TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 00 03 00 00 00 01; READ 0x0000 -> 5   ← the new port
Restore: `no scada modbus tcp server port` → "Port : 502", the port line gone from running-config;
CONNECT :5020 -> refused; CONNECT :502 -> ok, READ 0x0000 -> 5.
Verdict: PASS.

## STEP 2 — dynamic disable / enable (14:00:00)
`no scada modbus tcp server` → `show scada modbus tcp server`: "MODBUS is disabled";
CONNECT 10.38.215.10:502 -> FAILED (Connection refused).
`scada modbus tcp server` → "MODBUS is enabled / Write access: enabled / Port 502" (the
read-write access line survived the disable: running-config still `scada modbus tcp server
access read-write` + `scada modbus tcp server`); CONNECT -> ok, READ 0x0000 -> 5.
Verdict: PASS.

## STEP 3 — API disables then enables a port: port1.0.2 (member 1, index 1: 0x500d current, 0x500e configured) (14:00:21)
port1.0.2 was chosen because it is LINKED (x230 port1.0.3, in static-channel-group 2), so the
CURRENT-state word changes too; port1.0.1 (no link) already proved the same write in T22654.
  before: READ 0x500d -> 0xf103 (UP, full, auto, 1000); READ 0x500e -> 0xf000 (admin up, all auto)
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 50 0e 00 00   WRITE 0x500e = 0x0000 -> EXCEPTION ExceptionResponse(dev_id=1, …)
  5 s later: READ 0x500d -> 0x0000; READ 0x500e -> 0x0000
  CLI 14:00:31: "Link is DOWN, administrative state is DOWN"; status "disabled 1 auto auto
  1000BASE-T"; running-config interface port1.0.2 gained ` shutdown` (static-channel-group 2 kept)
  DUT log 01:01:04: "NSM[609]: Port down notification received for port1.0.2"; "port1.0.2 user
  shutdown"; "modbusd[531]: MODBUS: Failed to set Auto duplex for port1.0.2."; "MODBUS: Failed to
  set Auto polarity for port1.0.2."; "MODBUS: Failed to set Auto speed for port1.0.2."; "MODBUS:
  Performed write request from 10.38.215.1:23725: Write Single Register(FC=0x06) to member 1
  starting at port1.0.2 Config State(REG=0x500e) for 1 word with data 0x0000"
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 50 0e f0 00   WRITE 0x500e = 0xf000 -> EXCEPTION (same reason)
  10 s later: READ 0x500d -> 0xf103; READ 0x500e -> 0xf000
  CLI 14:00:55: "Link is UP, administrative state is UP"; status "connected 1 a-full a-1000";
  `shutdown` line gone.
Server counters at the end: single-register writes 20, "slave device failure 2" (these two).
Verdict: PASS — the port was disabled and re-enabled via the API and the CLI/registers reflect
it. Observation: because port1.0.2 is an aggregator member, the duplex/polarity/speed nibbles of
the same word cannot be applied (`% Cannot configure aggregator member`, orient-dt §2), so the
DUT applies the state nibble, logs three "Failed to set Auto …" lines and answers exception 4 —
the client sees an error for a write that did what it asked. On the non-member port1.0.1 the
identical write answers ok (T22654). Bench artefact (every linked stack port here is a LAG
member), reported not judged.

